      "Short label sweetened only with Reb M, per Target's listing",
      "Grass-fed whey isolate with a digestive enzyme blend",
      "Newer formula is xylitol-free"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Only 20 g of protein per scoop",
      "About $2.66 to $3.12 per 25 g, among the highest here",
      "Older xylitol formula still appears at some retailers"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A clean-label cake batter for lighter shakes, at a premium per gram.",
    "specs": {
      "Protein per serving": "20 g (label)",
      "Calories": "About 100-110",
      "Sweeteners": "Reb M (stevia-derived)",
      "Best for": "Short ingredient list"
    }
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-of/`,
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Birthday Cake Protein Powder",
          item: `${SITE_URL}/best-birthday-cake-protein-powder/`,
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best birthday cake protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most people, Dymatize ISO100 in Pebbles Birthday Cake. Per the label it gives 25 g of protein for 120 calories and 1 g of sugar per 30 g scoop, uses only whey isolate and is Informed Choice certified per the brand. PEScience Cake Pop is better for baking, and RYSE Loaded Birthday Cake is the cheapest per gram at the prices we saw."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does birthday cake protein powder have real sprinkles?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most do not. ISO100, PEScience, Legion, RYSE and Clean Simple Eats get their flavor from natural and artificial flavors only. RAW CBUM Itholate Birthday Cake is the exception: its label lists multicolored granules made from sugar, corn syrup, corn starch and food dyes, which add color, a slight crunch and about 2 g of sugar per scoop."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is there a birthday cake protein powder without sucralose?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. Legion Whey+ Birthday Cake is sweetened with stevia, per the brand, and the current Clean Simple Eats Cake Batter formula lists Reb M, a stevia-derived sweetener, as its only sweetener on Target's listing. Both are grass-fed whey isolates. Expect a lighter flavor than sucralose-sweetened tubs and, for some people, a slight stevia aftertaste."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between birthday cake and cake batter protein?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Loosely, birthday cake flavors lean toward frosting and sprinkles, while cake batter leans toward buttery raw batter. In practice the protein base matters more: casein blends like PEScience taste thicker and more batter-like, while isolates taste lighter. Read the protein source and sweetener lines rather than relying on the flavor name to predict taste."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is birthday cake protein powder good for weight loss?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can fit a weight loss plan. ISO100 and Legion deliver 22 to 25 g of protein for about 120 calories with little sugar, per their labels, and protein helps with fullness. A powder only supports weight loss inside an overall calorie deficit, and dessert flavors are easy to overuse, so measure scoops rather than pouring by eye."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I bake with birthday cake protein powder?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes, and it is one of the best flavors for it. Use a whey and casein blend such as PEScience Cake Pop for mug cakes and muffins, replace no more than about a third of the flour, add yogurt or banana for moisture, and take the bake out early. Pure isolates work better in no-bake recipes, yogurt and overnight oats."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is birthday cake protein powder safe for kids?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "The powders here are made for adults, and most children get enough protein from food. CBUM's sprinkles also contain synthetic dyes some families avoid. If you are considering protein powder for a child or teen, check with a pediatrician or registered dietitian first, especially if they have allergies, kidney concerns or take medication. This is not medical advice."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: SITE_NAME, url: SITE_URL },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.brand ?? product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Protein Powder Rankings",
    tags: [
      "best birthday cake protein powder",
      "birthday cake protein powder",
      "cake batter protein powder",
      "cake pop protein powder"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Birthday Cake Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Six birthday cake and cake batter powders you can buy now, checked against the label for that exact flavor, with the sweeteners, the sprinkles and the cost per 25 g of protein called out.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#compared">Compared at a Glance</a>
      <a href="#what-makes-it-cake">What Makes It Taste Like Cake</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#cake-vs-batter">Birthday Cake vs Cake Batter</a>
      <a href="#sweeteners">Sweeteners and Sugar</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#buyer-feedback">What Buyers Say</a>
      <a href="#recipes">Recipes and Uses</a>
      <a href="#not-ranked">Flavors We Did Not Rank</a>
      <a href="#who-should-buy">Who Should Buy It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          The best birthday cake protein powder for most people is Dymatize ISO100 in Pebbles Birthday Cake: per Dymatize's label it gives 25 g of protein, 120 calories and 1 g of sugar per 30 g scoop, with whey isolate as the only protein. PEScience Select Cake Pop is the better pick for baking and thick shakes, RYSE Loaded Birthday Cake is the cheapest per gram of protein on this list, Legion Whey+ and Clean Simple Eats are the options without sucralose, and RAW's CBUM Itholate is the one with actual sprinkle-style granules in the tub. Birthday cake is a vanilla-plus flavor, so the differences between these tubs come down to sweetener, protein base and price more than to the cake itself.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="compared" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Birthday Cake Protein Powders Compared at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            All six picks side by side. Protein and calories are per labeled serving, which is one scoop for every powder here. Where a brand does not publish a full panel for this exact flavor in text, the figures come from retailer or nutrition database copies of the label and are marked as approximate. Every number is per the label at the time of writing and can change between label versions, so check the panel on the tub you buy.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweeteners</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Cost per 25 g</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dymatize ISO100 Pebbles Birthday Cake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.52</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">PEScience Select Cake Pop</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, acesulfame K</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.12</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">RYSE Loaded Jet-Puffed Birthday Cake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Check panel</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $1.85</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Legion Whey+ Birthday Cake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">22 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Stevia</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.42</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">RAW CBUM Itholate Birthday Cake</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">25 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">120</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Sucralose, plus sugar in sprinkles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $3.00</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean Simple Eats Cake Batter</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 100-110</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Reb M (stevia-derived)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $2.66-$3.12</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            The headline differences are protein per scoop and sweetener. ISO100 and RYSE both reach 25 g per scoop, with ISO100 doing it in the smallest scoop thanks to its pure isolate base. PEScience lands at 24 g with a whey and casein blend that thickens more in a shaker. Legion and Clean Simple Eats give up a few grams of protein per scoop in exchange for labels without sucralose or acesulfame potassium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Price spreads more than you might expect for one flavor. RYSE is the cheapest per 25 g of protein at the price we saw, at about $1.85, while CBUM Itholate and the 20-serving Clean Simple Eats bag sit near $3.00. That gap is roughly $35 to $40 over a month of daily shakes, which is worth knowing before you pay extra for a sprinkle-filled tub you may only use on weekends.
          </p>

          <p>
            One number the table leaves out is sugar from sprinkles. CBUM Itholate is the only pick whose ingredient list includes multicolored granules made from sugar and corn syrup, and its panel shows 2 g of total sugar per scoop. Every other tub on this list gets its birthday cake character from flavoring alone and lists about 1 g of sugar or less. The section on sweeteners below breaks down what each brand uses and why it matters for taste.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-makes-it-cake" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Makes Protein Powder Taste Like Birthday Cake?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Birthday cake flavor is not one ingredient. In food flavoring it usually means a sweet vanilla base with buttery notes, a hint of almond or marzipan, and a sugary frosting character that some brands push toward cotton candy. On a protein label, all of that sits inside one line: natural flavors, or natural and artificial flavors. That is why two birthday cake powders can taste quite different even when their ingredient lists look almost identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dymatize ISO100 Pebbles Birthday Cake is a good example. Its ingredient list, as shown by Vitacost at the time of writing, is hydrolyzed whey protein isolate, whey protein isolate, natural and artificial flavors, and less than 2% of salt, soy lecithin, sucralose and steviol glycosides. There is no cereal and no cake in the tub. The Pebbles branding tells you what the flavor house was aiming for, not what you are eating.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sprinkles are the one real food ingredient some brands add. RAW's CBUM Itholate Birthday Cake lists multicolored granules made from sugar, corn syrup, corn grit, corn starch, palm oil and food dyes, including Red 40, Yellow 5, Yellow 6 and Blue 1. Those granules give the shake visible color and a slight crunch if you stir instead of blending. They also add a little sugar, which is the trade-off for the look.
          </p>

          <p>
            Salt does quiet but important work. Most birthday cake labels include a small amount of salt or sea salt, which sharpens sweetness and rounds off the bitterness some buyers notice in high-intensity sweeteners. It is the same trick bakers use when they add a pinch of salt to frosting. If a birthday cake powder tastes flat to you, a pinch of salt in a blended shake often fixes it faster than adding more powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein base changes the flavor too. Pure isolates such as ISO100 and Legion taste lighter and cleaner, closer to a vanilla frosting. Blends that include casein, such as PEScience Select, taste creamier and more like cake batter because casein thickens and carries flavor longer. If you have tried a birthday cake powder and found it thin, the base protein is a likelier cause than the flavoring, which is something our <a href="/best-tasting-protein-powder/">best tasting protein powder</a> guide covers across flavors.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Birthday Cake Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ranked by label quality for the birthday cake or cake batter flavor specifically, then by protein per scoop, sweetener and cost per 25 g of protein. Nutrition figures are per the label at the time of writing and can change between label versions; prices are those we saw on the day we checked. We have not taste-tested these powders; taste notes are buyer feedback reported as such.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="cake-vs-batter" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Birthday Cake vs Cake Batter vs Cake Pop: Is There a Difference?
          </h2>

          <p>
            Brands use birthday cake, cake batter, cake pop and frosted cupcake almost interchangeably, but there are loose differences worth knowing. Birthday cake flavors tend to lean toward frosting and sprinkles, with a brighter, sweeter profile. Cake batter flavors lean toward raw vanilla batter, with more butter and a richer, thicker finish. Cake pop sits between the two, a frosted cake taste in a creamier base.
          </p>

          <p>
            In practice the protein base matters more than the name. Clean Simple Eats Cake Batter is a grass-fed whey isolate sweetened with Reb M, so it drinks lighter than the name suggests. PEScience Cake Pop uses a whey isolate and casein blend, so it tastes closer to batter in a shake, even though the name says cake pop. Read the protein source line before deciding which style you are buying.
          </p>

          <p>
            Cereal-branded versions are a third group. Dymatize ISO100 Pebbles Birthday Cake and RYSE's Jet-Puffed Birthday Cake are licensed collaborations that borrow a cereal or marshmallow brand's take on birthday cake. Buyers tend to describe these as sweeter and more candy-like than a plain vanilla cake flavor. If you liked Dymatize's other cereal flavors, our <a href="/fruity-pebbles-protein-powder/">Fruity Pebbles protein powder</a> page covers that line in detail.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vanilla is the safe fallback. If you are not sure you will like a birthday cake powder, a good vanilla plus a few drops of butter or almond extract gets you most of the way there, and you keep the flexibility to use the tub in coffee or oatmeal. Our <a href="/best-vanilla-protein-powder/">best vanilla protein powder</a> guide lists the picks that work best as a blank base for that kind of adjustment.
          </p>

          <p>
            Limited editions complicate things. Several of the best-known birthday cake protein flavors over the last few years were seasonal or anniversary releases, which is why a flavor you remember may not be on sale today. We only ranked flavors that a major retailer or the brand itself listed as available on the day we checked, and we list the notable ones we left out further down the page.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sweeteners" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Sweeteners and Sugar in Birthday Cake Protein Powder
          </h2>

          <p>
            Birthday cake is a sweet flavor, so sweetener choice shapes the taste more here than it does in chocolate or unflavored powders. Four of our six picks rely on sucralose, sometimes paired with stevia or acesulfame potassium. Two avoid both: Legion Whey+ is stevia-sweetened per the brand, and the current Clean Simple Eats Cake Batter formula lists Reb M, a stevia-derived sweetener, as its only sweetener on Target's listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sucralose-sweetened powders taste closest to a classic frosted cake to most buyers, because sucralose has a clean, sugar-like sweetness without much aftertaste. Stevia can carry a slight licorice or bitter finish that some people notice more in light, vanilla-based flavors than in chocolate. Reb M is a purer stevia component that many buyers find less bitter, which may be why Clean Simple Eats switched to it in its newer formula.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar is low across the board. ISO100 lists 1 g of total sugar with 0 g added, PEScience lists 1 g, and Legion and Clean Simple Eats are marketed with no added sugar. CBUM Itholate lists 2 g of total sugar, coming from the sugar and corn syrup in its sprinkle granules. None of these would move the needle on blood sugar for most people, but they add up if you drink several shakes a day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Formulas change, so check the bag. Clean Simple Eats Cake Batter previously used xylitol, stevia and monk fruit; Vitamin Shoppe still lists that older version, with 4.5 g of xylitol per serving, while Target's listing shows the newer xylitol-free formula. Xylitol is toxic to dogs, so if you have one and buy an older bag, store it securely. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-without-artificial-sweeteners/">protein powder without artificial sweeteners</a> guide covers the wider options.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you react to sugar alcohols or high-intensity sweeteners, start with half a scoop. Gas and bloating from sweeteners are dose-related and differ widely between people. If you have a diagnosed digestive condition, diabetes or a medical diet, ask your doctor or a registered dietitian which sweeteners suit you, since this guide is not medical advice.
          </p>
        </div>

