        <div id="who-should-buy" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Who Should Buy Birthday Cake Protein (and Who Should Not)
          </h2>

          <p>
            Birthday cake protein suits people who struggle to finish plain shakes. If a dessert flavor is what gets you to hit your protein target, the small flavor premium is money well spent. It also suits anyone who uses protein in baking, yogurt and oats more than in water, because the flavor reads as a treat in food in a way that chocolate or vanilla sometimes does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            It suits people less if they use one tub for everything. A birthday cake powder is hard to put in coffee, savory smoothies or green shakes. If you want one tub for every use, a neutral vanilla is a safer buy. Many buyers end up with a plain everyday tub and a smaller birthday cake tub or bag as a second flavor, which also stops flavor fatigue.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lactose and dairy matter. Every pick here is dairy-based. The pure isolates, ISO100, Legion, CBUM and Clean Simple Eats, are very low in lactose, which many people with mild lactose intolerance tolerate well. PEScience's casein portion and RYSE's blend may carry more. If you need to avoid dairy entirely, none of these will work; our <a href="/best-lactose-free-protein-powder/">lactose-free protein powder</a> guide covers dairy-free options.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dyes are worth a note for parents. CBUM's sprinkle granules contain Red 40, Yellow 5, Yellow 6 and Blue 1, which some families prefer to avoid. Most children meet their protein needs from food and do not need protein powder at all, so check with a pediatrician before giving one to a child. Our <a href="/best-protein-powder-for-kids/">protein powder for kids</a> guide explains when it can make sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Certification matters if you are tested. Dymatize states ISO100 is Informed Choice certified, and Legion says it publishes third-party lab testing. We did not find a sport certification listed for CBUM Itholate, RYSE Loaded or Clean Simple Eats on the retailer pages we checked. If you compete in a tested sport, verify the exact product in the certifier's database before buying. Anyone with kidney disease, who is pregnant, or who takes medication should talk to a doctor or registered dietitian before adding protein powder.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming the tub contains cake or cereal</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Most birthday cake powders get their flavor entirely from natural and artificial flavors. ISO100 Pebbles Birthday Cake contains no cereal, and only CBUM Itholate on our list has actual sprinkle granules. Read the ingredient list rather than the front of the tub if real inclusions, or avoiding them, matters to you.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Judging the flavor in water only</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Birthday cake is built on vanilla and butter notes that come through much more clearly in milk. Many buyers who call a powder weak or thin in water find it tastes like cake in milk or a blended shake with ice. Test a scoop in milk before deciding a tub is a dud and leaving it in the cupboard.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing price per tub instead of per gram</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A $42 bag can cost more per gram of protein than a $50 tub. Clean Simple Eats at 20 g per scoop in a 20-serving bag works out near $2.66 to $3.12 per 25 g, while RYSE at 25 g in 27 servings is about $1.85. Divide price by total protein, not by servings, before you compare.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring sweeteners and sugar alcohols</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Birthday cake is a sweet flavor, so brands lean on sucralose, acesulfame potassium, stevia or sugar alcohols. If one of these upsets your stomach or tastes bitter to you, it will be more obvious here than in chocolate. Check the sweetener line, and note that older Clean Simple Eats bags contain xylitol.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Baking with a pure isolate and expecting cake</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pure whey isolate dries out and turns rubbery in the oven. For mug cakes and muffins, use a blend with casein such as PEScience, replace no more than about a third of the flour with powder, add a wet ingredient like yogurt, and take the bake out a minute or two early.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

